## First-time setup

```sh
./tools/bootstrap.sh
```

Clones the pinned third-party submodules these boards need. (Plain
`git submodule update --init` won't work here — a generated project has
`.gitmodules` but no git history, so there are no gitlinks for git to act on.)

## Build (target)

```sh
make
```
{% if boards | length > 1 %}
That builds `{{ boards[0] }}`. Pick another board from the table above with
`make BOARD=<preset>`, or build them all with `make all-boards`. Each board
builds into its own `build/<preset>/` directory, so they don't clobber each other.
{% endif %}
Output lands in `build/<preset>/{{ project_slug }}.elf` (plus `.bin`, `.hex`, `.map`).
`make help` lists every target.

The Makefile is a thin wrapper around the CMake presets. The equivalent by hand:

```sh
cmake --preset {{ boards[0] }}
cmake --build --preset {{ boards[0] }}
```

## Build & run tests (host)

Requires `ruby` on PATH (CMock uses it to generate mocks). Host tests are
board-independent — they exercise `src/app/` against mocked drivers.

```sh
make test
```

By hand:

```sh
cmake -S test -B build/host-test
cmake --build build/host-test
ctest --test-dir build/host-test
```

## Flash

Requires `openocd` on PATH and an ST-Link probe.

```sh
make flash{% if boards | length > 1 %}                      # or: make flash BOARD=<preset>{% endif %}
```

This rebuilds if needed, then programs, verifies and resets the board. For a
different probe, configure with `-DOPENOCD_INTERFACE=<name>` (any config under
OpenOCD's `interface/`). Under the hood it is the CMake `flash` target,
`cmake --build --preset <preset> --target flash`, which runs:

```sh
{% for b in boards -%}
# {{ b }}
openocd -f interface/stlink.cfg -f target/{{ board_meta[b]['openocd'] }}.cfg \
  -c "program build/{{ b }}/{{ project_slug }}.elf verify reset exit"
{% endfor %}```

